<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Total-conversion behavior: hide (almost) every vanilla/DLC leader so only
  Warmstone civs, plus exactly one vanilla leader for AI opponents to use, are
  available.

  REVISION 2: the original version only deleted the `Players` table (Frontend/
  Configuration database). Confirmed in-game (full relaunch, no change) that this
  was wrong - `Players` turned out to be display-only metadata (icons/ability
  text), not what actually gates which leaders are selectable. That real gate is
  believed to be `CivilizationLeaders` (Gameplay database), the table that pairs
  a LeaderType to a CivilizationType - deleting the pairing here rather than the
  `Civilizations`/`Leaders` catalog tables themselves is the more surgical option:
  it leaves every vanilla civ/leader's own data (Civilopedia entries, AI logic,
  etc.) fully intact, it just removes their availability as a human/AI pick.

  NOTE: this is still not fully verified without running the game - the actual
  enumeration logic lives in the compiled game engine, not anything readable in
  the Lua UI files. If this doesn't work either, the next thing to try would be
  the `Civilizations`/`Leaders` primary tables directly.

  Kept: CIVILIZATION_ROME / LEADER_TRAJAN, so AI opponents have at least one real
  option in a normal-sized game while only Stalwarts exists on the Warmstone side.
  Both re-added rows below use Rome/Trajan's own existing Firaxis LOC keys and
  icons verbatim (confirmed from the installed game's own
  Configuration/Data/Players.xml and Gameplay/Data/Civilizations.xml) - this is
  reusing already-shipped vanilla content, not new authored copy.

  Load-order matters: this file's actions are given a LOWER LoadOrder than any
  civ's own file in Warmstone.modinfo, so these deletes run first and each
  Warmstone civ's own rows (added by its own Civilization/Config files) survive.

  This only ever affects games created while this mod is enabled - Civ6 rebuilds
  the ruleset database fresh per game from currently-active content, so
  disabling the mod (or games that never had it enabled) is untouched.
-->
<GameInfo>
	<Players>
		<Delete/>
		<Row CivilizationType="CIVILIZATION_ROME" LeaderType="LEADER_TRAJAN" CivilizationName="LOC_CIVILIZATION_ROME_NAME" CivilizationIcon="ICON_CIVILIZATION_ROME" LeaderName="LOC_LEADER_TRAJAN_NAME" LeaderIcon="ICON_LEADER_TRAJAN" CivilizationAbilityName="LOC_TRAIT_CIVILIZATION_ALL_ROADS_TO_ROME_NAME" CivilizationAbilityDescription="LOC_TRAIT_CIVILIZATION_ALL_ROADS_TO_ROME_DESCRIPTION" CivilizationAbilityIcon="ICON_CIVILIZATION_ROME" LeaderAbilityName="LOC_TRAIT_LEADER_TRAJANS_COLUMN_NAME" LeaderAbilityDescription="LOC_TRAIT_LEADER_TRAJANS_COLUMN_DESCRIPTION" LeaderAbilityIcon="ICON_LEADER_TRAJAN"/>
	</Players>
	<CivilizationLeaders>
		<Delete/>
		<Row CivilizationType="CIVILIZATION_ROME" LeaderType="LEADER_TRAJAN" CapitalName="LOC_CITY_NAME_ROME"/>
	</CivilizationLeaders>
</GameInfo>
